# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-cad-source-loader-drops-a-second-form-on-a-line — a loaded file's later forms on a line were silently discarded
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: cad-runtime
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-cador-addattribute-argument-order
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

alfe's CAD-side source loader runs only the FIRST form of each line of a
loaded file and discards the rest, without a word. Through the
clautolisp-hosted protocol harness (no CAD needed), loading

#+begin_example
(princ "x")(princ "y")
(setq a 1)(setq b 2)
#+end_example

gave

#+begin_example
stdout:  "x(A 1 B UNBOUND)"
status:  DONE 1 OK      protocol/stderr.txt empty
#+end_example

=y= was never printed and =b= was never set. clautolisp's own loader
runs both forms (=xy= and =(A 1 B 2)=), so this was alfe's CAD-side
loader alone -- the one that runs inside BricsCAD and AutoCAD.

Two forms on a line is ordinary AutoLISP and a common style
(=(princ "…")(princ x)=), so this silently changed the meaning of user
code in every loaded file that has such a line.

* Cause

=autolisp-source-load-run-body-impl=
(autolisp-front-end/source/runtime/autolisp-bootstrap.lsp) accumulates
lines into =form-text= until =autolisp-source-scan-text= reports the text
BALANCED, then:

#+begin_src lisp
(setq form-read (vl-catch-all-apply 'read
                  (list (autolisp-source-trim-leading-junk form-text))))
… evaluate form-read …
(setq form-text "")          ;; <- everything after the first form is gone
#+end_src

=read= returns the FIRST form of the string. Balanced text can hold any
number of forms, and the buffer was cleared straight after the first, so
the remainder went with it.

* How it surfaced

Chasing a failure that looked swallowed: a loaded file with
=(setq ran 1)(/ 1 0)= on one line reported DONE OK. The division looked
lost in the reporting path, and was first written up as a re-entrancy
defect in the eval override
(issues/closed/alfe-eval-request-form-reenters-its-own-temp-file.issue,
now marked MISDIAGNOSED). Looking at the protocol directory settled it:
=alfe-eval-1.lsp= held =(setq *AUTOLISP-EVAL-RESULT* (SETQ RAN 1))= --
the first form -- so the division had never been evaluated at all.

* Resolved — clautolisp 2.2.100

Two changes in the bootstrap:

- =autolisp-source-first-form-end= (new): where the first top-level form
  of a string ends, as a 1-based index, or NIL. It follows the same
  conventions as =autolisp-source-scan-text= -- a string runs to its
  unescaped closing quote, a semicolon comments out the rest of its line
  -- and handles both a parenthesised list (ending at the paren that
  brings the depth back to zero) and a bare atom (ending at the first
  whitespace after it, or at end of text).
- the read loop's =complete= branch now LOOPS: it splits the first form
  off =form-text=, reads and evaluates that, re-scans the remainder and
  continues while another complete form is there. What is left at the end
  is either insubstantial (whitespace, a trailing comment), in which case
  the buffer is cleared as before, or the beginning of a form the next
  lines continue -- which is kept, and takes the current line as its
  start line so a later diagnostic points at the right place.

Tests (autolisp-front-end/tests/file-protocol-tests.lisp, hosted under
clautolisp):

- =protocol-loaded-file-runs-every-form-on-a-line= — two forms on line 1,
  two on line 2, then a form SPANNING lines 3-4: stdout must be
  =xyz(A 1 B 2)=. Before the fix it was =x(A 1 B UNBOUND)=.
- =protocol-failure-inside-a-loaded-file-fails-that-request= — the
  failure that started this: a file whose second form on a line signals
  must fail ITS request, at one nesting level and at two, with the
  engine's own message, and no failure attributed to a later request.

alfe suite 1182 checks, 0 failures.

* Note

This also completes acceptance criterion 2 of
=issues/open/alfe-autocad-error-primitive-masks-load-failure.issue= (a
nested loaded-file failure reaching alfe with the original text and the
inner source location), which is what that issue was waiting on besides
its SCHME+ verification.
